ROLE: novelty_killer  VOTE: yellow

CLOSEST_PRIOR_WORK:
- {cite: "duckdb/duckdb #15473 'Sniff CSV get column type wrong'", url: "https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues/15473", why_close: "Sample-driven mis-sniffing of a column's type — but the failure mode is the LOUD arm (ConversionException on CREATE/INSERT), tested at sample_size=-1, in-sample mis-detection. Confirms the loud arm is real & maintainer-acknowledged; does NOT report silent float→int truncation."}
- {cite: "duckdb/duckdb #10528 'CSV parsing double in scientific notation (1E-7) as bigint'", url: "https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues/10528", why_close: "Float-valued input sniffed as BIGINT (closest conceptual cousin), but it is WITHIN-sample mis-typing of scientific notation, closed/reproduced; does not concern post-sample silent truncation."}
- {cite: "duckdb/duckdb #11840 'Read CSV sniffer inconsistency depending on number/order of columns'", url: "https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues/11840", why_close: "Adjacent sniffer-instability family; not the silent-truncation mechanism."}
- {cite: "duckdb/duckdb #6756 auto_type_candidates", url: "https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb/issues/6756", why_close: "The documented mitigation knob for DOUBLE/BIGINT sniffing — relevant existing surface the authors must situate against."}

NAMING_VS_MECHANISM: Real mechanism delta, but a THIN one. "Sampling picks the wrong type" is pure folklore (renaming). The genuine delta is the demonstrated SILENT float→int truncation past the sample + the loud-vs-silent-BY-VALUE-TYPE asymmetry. That asymmetry is NOT renaming — it is a controlled, reproduced behavioral fact. BUT it decomposes into two individually-known behaviors: (1) sample-based type inference (folklore), and (2) standard SQL implicit narrowing cast where float→int truncates silently while string→int raises (also standard cast semantics). The "novelty" is the predictable intersection of two non-novel facts; the only element that is hard to derive a priori is the DOCUMENTED-PROMISE VIOLATION ("scanner will immediately stop and throw").

FATAL_COLLISIONS: None verified. Searched the DuckDB tracker via GitHub search API across 6 query variants (csv+sniff+float+integer+truncate; read_csv+sample_size+truncated; integer+column+decimal+lost; silently+rounded+integer; sniffer+sample+wrong+type+cast; BIGINT+DOUBLE+sample) → total_count=0 for the specific silent-truncation-past-sample phenomenon; the only hits (#10528/#15473/#11840) are in-sample mis-sniff or LOUD-error variants. No CVE. No collision verifiable in ≥2 sources → no RED.

MANDATORY_RELATED_WORK: #15473, #10528, #11840, #6756 (all above); DuckDB "CSV Sniffer" (2023) blog + "Reading Faulty CSV Files" docs page (the violated contract — must be cited as the promise being broken); and the general type-inference-from-sample hazard literature (pandas `low_memory` mixed-dtype warning lineage; Frictionless/CSV type-guessing) to honestly fence the folklore boundary the claim must clear.

RATIONALE: Two independent prior-art sources confirm the LOUD arm is genuine and tracked (#15473 ConversionException; #10528 BIGINT mis-typing) while ZERO sources report the SILENT float→int truncation past the sample — so the specific finding is un-owned and survives the novelty kill (not RED). I withhold GREEN because the mechanism, stripped of framing, is the foreseeable composition of sample-inference + standard SQL narrowing-cast semantics; the single element that genuinely escapes folklore is the self-contract violation, which is contract-bug-flavored (file-an-issue energy) rather than a new mechanism. That is precisely the gate-4 risk the orchestrator flagged. YELLOW: the claim is not collision-killable, but its novelty rests on composition + a doc-violation hook, not a new mechanism — L1 must demonstrate real-world blast-radius (the integer-early/float-later corpus + silent-corruption prevalence) to lift it above "known-hazard instance N." Without that, it converges as a high-quality bug report, not a paper.
